ount amounts and rounded only once at the end. Whenever a line's discount lands on a half cent, the two disagree. Example: two lines of 1.25 with 10% off. Each prints as 1.13, so the PDF sums to 2.26, but the total computed 2.25.\n\n**Fix** (`billing/totals.py`): `invoice_total()` now sums `line_totals(inv)` and applies tax to that sum. The public API is unchanged.\n\n**Regression tests** (`tests/test_billing.py`):\n- `test_total_matches_sum_of_printed_lines_with_half_cent_discounts`: the total equals the sum of the printed lines (2.26).\n- `test_total_matches_sum_of_printed_lines_with_tax`: the same check with 19% tax applied.\n\nI ran both new tests against the original `totals.py`, and both fail there. With the fix, the full suite passes: 5 passed, 0 failed.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- The tracked `.pyc` files under `billing/__pycache__/` and `tests/__pycache__/` show as modified because pytest rewrote them. I left them alone; you probably want to discard those changes or add `__pycache__` to `.gitignore`.\n- Nothing is committed.\n- The fix changes totals only for invoices where a discount produces a half-cent. Existing invoices that hit this case will now show a total one cent different from the stored value if it was recomputed. I didn't check whether any stored totals or downstream systems rely on the old value.", "ttft_ms": 806, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 24664, "uuid": "ac09d2a4-9216-4fa6-a143-fb0adc5a186c", "ttft_stream_ms": 616, "time_to_request_ms": 105, "first_content_frame_ms": 616, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0, "timestamp": "2026-10-09T15:49:32.559Z"}